These SVG files are Codicons, Visual Studio Code's icon set.

    Source:    https://github.com/microsoft/vscode-codicons
    Copyright: (c) Microsoft Corporation
    License:   CC BY 4.0 — https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

Fetched from src/icons/ in that repository, unmodified on disk. EVERY
FILE HERE KEEPS ITS UPSTREAM NAME, which is what makes that sentence
checkable rather than a claim: each one is src/icons/<same name>.svg and
is byte-identical to it. Verify with

    curl -s https://raw.githubusercontent.com/microsoft/vscode-codicons/\
main/src/icons/<name>.svg | diff - <name>.svg

The review of PR #426 is why this paragraph is this specific. book.svg
arrived hand-drawn, and the blanket "fetched, unmodified" above it was
then false for one of five files — a licensing statement is the wrong
place for an approximation, and the machine-generated coordinate
precision of its neighbours made the odd one out visible on sight. It
was replaced with the real Codicon rather than the claim being narrowed.

THE RECIPE ABOVE HAS BEEN RUN, most recently on 2026-09-09, against
every file in this directory and every file in components/toolbox/icons.
EVERY ONE matched src/icons/<same name>.svg on that repository's main
branch, byte for byte. A recipe nobody has executed is a claim like any
other, which is what the review of #426 said about the sentence it
verifies.

AND THE RESULT IS RECORDED AGAINST BYTES, NOT AGAINST A DATE — see
VERIFIED.sha256 beside this file, which lists every icon with the
SHA-256 of the bytes that were compared. That is issue #442's second
finding, and it was a fair one: this file recorded a one-time manual
check three paragraphs above its own argument that one-time manual
checks are exactly what rot. Both halves were true, which is what made
it worth fixing rather than deleting.

A date attached to a DIRECTORY silently covers whatever is added to it
afterwards, and something already had been. book.svg arrived in
d712a28c, the same commit that wrote the previous "VERIFIED 2026-08-30"
line, so the sentence was stamped two days before the file it covered
existed. Nothing there was dishonest — the paragraph above says
book.svg was re-fetched, and it had been — but nothing connected the
sentence to the bytes, so the next added icon would have inherited it
in silence.

TestTheVerifiedIconsAreStillTheVerifiedBytes fails the moment the
directory and VERIFIED.sha256 stop agreeing, in EITHER direction: an
icon with no line is one nobody compared, and a line with no icon is a
verification of a file that is gone. Re-run the diff above for whatever
it names, THEN update the line. Updating the line to make the test pass
without running the diff turns a checked claim back into an asserted
one, which is the whole thing this is fixing.

No count is written down, deliberately: a number in prose is a sample
taken once, and a licensing statement is the worst place to keep a stale
one. "Every file" stays true when an icon is added; "all 38" quietly
stops being a claim about the directory and becomes a claim about the
day it was typed. Count them with `ls *.svg | wc -l` if you need the
number.

Re-run the diff after adding an icon. Upstream's main branch moves, so a
future mismatch is as likely to be an upstream edit as a bad copy, and
the diff says which.

The activitybar package substitutes the documents' fill="currentColor"
for a concrete colour before rasterizing, which is a render-time
substitution and not a change to these files.

CC BY 4.0 permits use and modification, commercial included, and requires
attribution — which is what this file is. Keep it beside the icons.
